A||||3||DaveL||Auto Transmission Connecter||||1||
Z||000000||DaveL||09-15-2000||03:17 PM||||I have an 87 SE 2.5 DIS with THM125. I've noticed that there seems to be a large electrical connector going to very near where the shift cable attaches. What is this and how does it affect transmission performance? Also, it is so greasy and dirty I can't quite make out how it comes loose.||||reg||1
Z||000001||286SE'S||09-15-2000||04:11 PM||||If you are looking at the front of the trans then this is the lock-up solonoid. When you get to crusing speed it activates and gives the torque converter a break. If you press the gas hard it disengages. Failure of this device causes the car to stall when you come to a stop. Sort of like not pressing in the clutch on a standard.<BR>On the other hand you may be looking at the speed sensor that runs the speedometer/ECM.<BR>Hard to say without a greasy picture.<BR>Terry<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||09-15-2000||08:19 PM||||Three electrical connectors on TH125.<P>1. TC lockup. located on front of tranny. Not usually noticed by most people. Difficult to see most of the time.<P>2. VSS. plugs into small can shaped device (the VSS sender) near right side axle socket.<P>3. Neutral Start. Plugs into black box on shifter linkage above left axle outlet.<P>Neutral start has nothing to do with transmission performance. There is a white locking tab that must be removed before the latch can be undone to remove the connector. (The locking tab may have broken and fallen off.)<P>It's easier and safer to remove the whole NS switch from the tranny. Take the nut off the linkage, and then take out the screws that hold the switch to the tranny. This way you wont damage either the connector or switch trying to get it undone.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 09-15-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||DaveL||09-16-2000||07:59 AM||||Thanks for the replies. Next time I'll include a greasy picture. I have never posted any pix to this forum. I guess I'll have to figure out how to do it.<P>Anyway...... it was the neutral safety switch I've been looking at. It is a large electrical type connector right on the same bracket the shift cable is attached to.<BR>Thanks.:-b||||reg||1
